WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.150
Next, we will look at some Linux software
tools we can use to troubleshoot a problem.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.950
As we go through each of these, the key is
to learn when you would use each tool and

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.060
the syntax behind each tool.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.240
Many of these tools are similar to ones you
would see in Windows.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.320
First, we have a ping, which runs the same
as in Windows, where we are trying to get

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.730
to an IP address.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:25.619
I will type, ping 10.1.10.1, and select the
Enter key on the keyboard.

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:29.919
We see that we can reach this destination,
which happens to be a gateway router.

00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:34.780
After letting this run, I will select Ctrl+C
on the keyboard to end this command.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:43.780
Next, I will run the ip addr command, which
shows the IP address for each network adapter

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.089
on this device.

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:50.480
Traceroute is similar to tracert in Windows
as it shows a complete path from a source

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.030
to a destination.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:59.570
I will type, traceroute www.learnkey.com,
select the Enter key on the keyboard, and

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.089
we see the path of routers going from the
source to the destination.

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:10.260
Tracepath, which I will run next, shows the
actual addresses of each destination on the

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.610
path to a final destination.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:19.330
The dig command is similar to the nslookup
command in Windows, as the dig command when

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:25.471
I run it here shows the location of the current
DNS server plus the addresses of the 13 root

00:01:25.471 --> 00:01:28.820
domain servers around the world.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:32.330
The host command actually performs DNS lookups.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:39.280
For example, if I type, host google.com, and
select the Enter key on the keyboard, we see

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.670
the DNS servers for google.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:47.140
The netstat command, just like the one in
Windows, shows open and listening ports on

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.510
a device.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.670
For this, I do not need to run netstat with
any attributes.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.950
I can just run netstat.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:00.740
Lastly, the arp command does in Linux what
it does in Windows: Show addresses and their

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.100
accompanying MAC addresses.

